	<aside style="clear: none;">So, this is my second annual birthday post. And, like last year, I am actually writing it more than
		three weeks afterwards.</aside>

	<p>The number 74 holds intriguing mathematical and cultural significance. 
		Mathematically, 74 is an even composite number, divisible by 1, 2, 37, and itself. 
		It serves as an example of the relationships between prime factors, as it is the 
		product of 2 and 37, two prime numbers. In coding and cryptography, numbers like 74 
		often appear due to their unique properties within various algorithms and sequences. 
		Culturally, 74 carries importance in different contexts; for example, it is the 
		atomic number of tungsten, a highly valued element for its strength and resistance 
		to heat. The number also resonates symbolically&mdash;sometimes representing perseverance 
		and stability. And, it's the age I turned today.</p>

    <p id='Extract'>To be honest, last year at this time I wasn't at all convinced I would make it another
		year. (I thought the same, the year before.) I hadn't yet gotten my hip replacement 
		scheduled, and every step I attempted was exhausting and
		painful. My blood pressure was very low (except when it was very high); my electrolytes were out of balance
		and my body had swollen with edema to the point of serious discomfort.</p>

	<p>But I perservered&mdash;what else can ya do? My only other choice was to lie down
		until I finally stopped breathing. But my brain doesn't shut off that easily. So I continued
		to show up at doctor appointments, taking charge of my own health rather than wait for
		a doctor to come up with something on their own.</p>

	<p>The magic bullet turned out to be potassium. One of my medications caused my body to lose potassium,
		but no provision had been made to replace it. Once I started supplementing it,
		everything else fell into place. It's all about the electrolyte balances, people!
		Your calcium/magnesium/phosphorus have to balance; and your sodium/potassium has to balance.
		They tell you to avoid salt (sodium), but you can achieve the same balance by adding potassium.
		And it really has made a major difference.
		I've been able to go for walks with the dogs around our new neighborhood.</p>

	<p>(Okay, to be honest, if <i>I</i> am holding their leashes, I can't go very far. But my
		daughter, Jenny, has been encouraging me to go for a walk with them, while she and grandson
		Dominic actually hold the leashes.)</p>

    <img src="01.jpg"
		alt="Being an ADULT is pretty easy, you just feel tired all the time and tell people abpout how tired you are and they tell you how tired they are." />

	<p>But let's look quickly at how I spent this previous year.</p>

	<p>Now, understand, in the background was me trying to get my hip replacement arranged. First,
		convincing the insurance company I needed it, since last year's spinal surgery hadn't helped.
		Then, trying to get it scheduled. All of which was going on for months before I finally had the
		surgery in August.</p>

	<p>Without it, I couldn't cross my legs to cut my own toenails. My daughter, Jenny,
		made sure they got taken care of.</p>

    <img src="02.jpg" />

	<p>And then, I went with Jenny and the grandkids to Kaua'i to celebrate my oldest
		grandson's 25th birthday. And yes, walking's tough but I can still swim!</p>
	
    <img src="03.jpg" />

	<p>I returned to Hawaii on my own in August, a week before my scheduled surgery.
		And I car camped, and revisited most of the places I'd loved when I lived here, though I couldn't
		quite fit in a visit to Hana where I lived.</p>

    <img src="04.jpg" />

	<p>And then I underwent the hip replacement on August 22. The surgery was considered a success,
		and I was supposed to immediately start physical therapy. However, my insurance company
		(Banner Medicare Advantage Plus) took months to okay it, so I did not get the benefit of
		that, which would probably have helped me heal more thoroughly.</p>

    <img src="05.jpg" />

	<p>Nevertheless, I flew to Florida for my 55th high school reunion, where I had a really good time
		despite needing a cane to get around. And I was happy to see that, among us survivors, at least,
		we're all still looking pretty healthy.</p>

    <img src="06.jpg" />

	<p>I also made a car camping trip through Sedona and Grand Canyon. So, looking back,
		I have to admit that I haven't really let my hip issues slow me down.</p>

	<p>2024/2025, of course, is the period where AI really took off. I've been working with
		it a lot, especially getting it to create generic images to order for old blog posts
		that, originally, had no illustrations at all. I've also been using it as a programming
		amenuensis. I will have to let others worry about the issues regarding training on
		copyrighted works. Personally, I don't care if they train on my web site, because that's
		probably as close to immortality as I can hope for. But that's me.</p>

	<p>Meanwhile, I've also been using it to restore and colorize old family photos,
		like this one of my dad and his siblings.</p>

    <img src="13.jpg" />

	<p>Probably the worst to happen (other than the election itself) was Elon Musk's descent into
		madness. I still love my Tesla, leased before anyone really knew who Musk even was.
		And I love the self-driving feature, even though it costs me an extra $99 a month.
		At my age, I think it's safe to say my car can drive more safely than I can.
		Of course, I do have to stay alert&mdash;the car enforces it&mdash;and every once in
		while I do have to correct it, for example if there's a driveway very close to my turn,
		it may be fooled into trying to turn into the driveway. But it's easy to recover.
		However, I did feel the need to make my position known.</p>

    <img src="07.jpg" />
    <img src="08.jpg" />

	<p>Then, sometime in November, another daughter, Karen, who lives in Virginia, sent
		me something very special. It's a "smart oven"! And it comes with a subscription
		to 4 high-quality meals a week, which she is paying for.</p>

    <img src="09.jpg" />

	<p>I'm basically lazy. While I love to cook wonderful meals for people (whether family or
		visitors), if I'm only cooking for myself, I generally just head for Marie Callendar's.
		It's hard to beat zero prep, almost instant cooking, and pretty decent ingredients
		and taste.</p>

	<p>But these <a href="https://my.tovala.com/">Tovala oven</a> meals require <i>almost</i>
		zero prep; and they generally cook in about 20 minutes, which means I can start my dinner,
		feed the dogs, and then have mine shortly after they finish. And they are, for the
		most part, restaurant quality.</p>

